1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,460
The prime numbers thin out as they grow.

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:08,900
Up to x there are about x over log x
of them, and the logarithmic integral,

3
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:12,600
Li of x,
follows their count far more closely.

4
00:00:13,030 --> 00:00:18,480
In 1859 Riemann tied the error to the
zeros of one function, the zeta function.

5
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:23,060
His hypothesis says where those zeros lie.
If it holds,

6
00:00:23,420 --> 00:00:27,680
the error stays below a constant times
the square root of x, times log x,

7
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:28,730
for large x.

8
00:00:29,420 --> 00:00:31,960
On 20 November 2026, L.

9
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:36,080
Alpöge published a short note claiming
a proof of the Riemann hypothesis.

10
00:00:37,220 --> 00:00:39,450
The note says the proof was
found working with Claude.

11
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:43,820
With the note came a proof that
has since been checked by machine:

12
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:48,780
the full build passes,
it uses only Lean's three standard axioms,

13
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:51,470
and several independent kernels accept it.

14
00:00:54,817 --> 00:00:57,757
Where the real part of
s is greater than one,

15
00:00:58,477 --> 00:01:01,677
zeta of s is the sum of
one over n to the power s.

16
00:01:02,437 --> 00:01:06,977
It extends to the whole complex plane,
with a single pole, at s equals one.

17
00:01:07,377 --> 00:01:13,257
It vanishes at minus two, minus four,
minus six, and so on: the trivial zeros.

18
00:01:14,277 --> 00:01:16,357
Every other zero lies
in the critical strip,

19
00:01:16,757 --> 00:01:18,997
where the real part is
between zero and one.

20
00:01:19,467 --> 00:01:22,477
Here is the size of zeta over the strip,
computed.

21
00:01:23,117 --> 00:01:26,287
The first zeros sit at heights 14.13,

22
00:01:26,287 --> 00:01:29,777
21.02 and 25.01,

23
00:01:30,257 --> 00:01:32,597
each on the line where
the real part is one half.

24
00:01:33,097 --> 00:01:37,867
The Riemann hypothesis says that every
non-trivial zero lies on this line.

25
00:01:38,367 --> 00:01:43,137
Mathlib, the mathematical library written
by Lean's community, states it this way:

26
00:01:43,877 --> 00:01:48,217
every s where zeta vanishes, other
than minus two, minus four and so on,

27
00:01:48,417 --> 00:01:50,927
and other than one,
has real part one half.

28
00:01:53,983 --> 00:01:59,603
The Lean source is large:
175,187 files in ten libraries.

29
00:02:01,063 --> 00:02:04,113
Its root file, Main.lean,
exports five theorems.

30
00:02:04,423 --> 00:02:07,023
The first has as its type
exactly Mathlib's proposition,

31
00:02:07,543 --> 00:02:11,803
the Riemann hypothesis. The second states
the same proposition in the source's own

32
00:02:11,803 --> 00:02:12,803
words.

33
00:02:13,123 --> 00:02:15,283
The other three concern
Dirichlet L-functions.

34
00:02:15,743 --> 00:02:20,463
A Dirichlet character modulo N, chi,
is multiplicative, repeats with period N,

35
00:02:20,683 --> 00:02:22,983
and is zero on numbers
that share a factor with N.

36
00:02:23,503 --> 00:02:27,843
Its L-function is the sum of chi of n
over n to the s, continued like zeta.

37
00:02:28,963 --> 00:02:30,273
Zeta is the case N equals one.

38
00:02:30,843 --> 00:02:32,823
All five theorems rest on one proposition:

39
00:02:33,503 --> 00:02:37,683
no Dirichlet L-function vanishes where the
real part of s is greater than one half,

40
00:02:38,243 --> 00:02:40,653
except at s equals one
for a principal character.

41
00:02:41,373 --> 00:02:45,203
The step from it to the five theorems
is short and classical. For zeta,

42
00:02:45,403 --> 00:02:49,403
the functional equation mirrors any
non-trivial zero left of the line to a

43
00:02:49,403 --> 00:02:50,403
zero right of it.

44
00:02:52,983 --> 00:02:55,843
Below that proposition,
the proof has two halves.

45
00:02:56,443 --> 00:02:59,183
The analytic half works with
primes through lambda of n,

46
00:02:59,603 --> 00:03:05,403
the von Mangoldt function: log p when n is
a power of a prime p, and zero otherwise.

47
00:03:06,283 --> 00:03:11,483
The source sums lambda of n times
x over n minus one, for n up to x,

48
00:03:11,703 --> 00:03:16,143
then subtracts x log x and
adds one plus gamma, times x;

49
00:03:16,783 --> 00:03:19,933
gamma is Euler's constant.
Call this G of x.

50
00:03:20,463 --> 00:03:22,803
Classically, G has an explicit formula:

51
00:03:23,523 --> 00:03:26,783
each non-trivial zero rho
of zeta contributes a wave,

52
00:03:27,343 --> 00:03:29,723
x to the rho over rho times rho minus one.

53
00:03:30,293 --> 00:03:35,923
Here is the exact G, computed, against the
waves of the first zeros. With more zeros,

54
00:03:36,203 --> 00:03:37,983
they follow G ever more closely.

55
00:03:38,553 --> 00:03:42,143
A zero with real part beta makes
a wave of size x to the beta.

56
00:03:42,763 --> 00:03:46,303
If every zero is on the line,
G stays of size root x.

57
00:03:46,303 --> 00:03:49,943
A zero to the right of the
line would force larger swings.

58
00:03:50,543 --> 00:03:55,103
The source turns this around, for suitable
finite families of Dirichlet characters,

59
00:03:55,103 --> 00:04:01,063
with their weighted prime sum. Take theta
between zero and one, and delta positive.

60
00:04:01,903 --> 00:04:07,863
If the sum stays below a constant times
y to the theta on windows from Y to Y to

61
00:04:07,863 --> 00:04:10,803
the one plus delta,
for arbitrarily large Y,

62
00:04:11,283 --> 00:04:14,553
then no L-function of the
family vanishes right of theta.

63
00:04:15,123 --> 00:04:20,503
The bound is one-sided and needed
only on sparse windows. Classically,

64
00:04:21,063 --> 00:04:25,243
one-sided bounds exclude zeros through
positivity, as in Landau's theorem;

65
00:04:25,903 --> 00:04:29,193
which positivity the source uses,
the article leaves open.

66
00:04:29,863 --> 00:04:33,483
The criterion is fed by a square-root
bound stated in the source:

67
00:04:34,383 --> 00:04:37,063
on the windows of suitably
compatible auxiliary families,

68
00:04:37,503 --> 00:04:42,283
the sum eventually stays below x to the
one half plus a small multiple of epsilon.

69
00:04:42,283 --> 00:04:45,923
The difficulty moves into
producing those families.

70
00:04:48,983 --> 00:04:52,583
Here is the top of the proof,
as the source writes it.

71
00:04:53,903 --> 00:04:57,243
The Riemann hypothesis rests
on the every-character theorem.

72
00:04:58,063 --> 00:05:02,863
On the left is the analytic half;
on the right, the automorphic half,

73
00:05:03,283 --> 00:05:04,583
which supplies the families.

74
00:05:05,203 --> 00:05:10,783
Each box is one Lean declaration. Beneath
them lie the ten libraries; the largest,

75
00:05:11,403 --> 00:05:16,153
Development, has about 142,000
files and 12 million lines.

76
00:05:16,753 --> 00:05:21,563
The automorphic half rests on four
main inputs, named here but not proved.

77
00:05:22,043 --> 00:05:26,183
First, the window bound:
lengths of Tate–Shafarevich groups, Sha,

78
00:05:26,563 --> 00:05:30,743
from Galois cohomology, bounded over
fields cut out of cyclotomic fields,

79
00:05:31,123 --> 00:05:32,373
the fields of roots of unity.

80
00:05:32,773 --> 00:05:35,643
Second,
Galois representations for polarized,

81
00:05:36,343 --> 00:05:39,943
regular algebraic cuspidal
representations over CM fields. Third,

82
00:05:40,283 --> 00:05:43,863
a form of the local Langlands
correspondence for GL n. Fourth,

83
00:05:44,603 --> 00:05:47,133
theta lifts between unitary groups,
in a stable range.

84
00:05:47,603 --> 00:05:51,083
Each has the shape of published theorems.
Except for local Langlands,

85
00:05:51,603 --> 00:05:55,533
how the stated form relates to them is a
question the article leaves to experts.

86
00:05:55,993 --> 00:05:59,103
Both halves meet in one theorem:
the families arrive,

87
00:05:59,543 --> 00:06:01,323
the square-root bound holds on windows,

88
00:06:01,723 --> 00:06:05,843
and the criterion clears every primitive
character's L-function right of one half.

89
00:06:06,583 --> 00:06:09,193
Zeta is simply the case
of the trivial character.

90
00:06:12,250 --> 00:06:13,910
As the note puts it,

91
00:06:14,230 --> 00:06:19,310
Stepanov's proof of the Riemann hypothesis
for curves over finite fields uses a

92
00:06:19,310 --> 00:06:24,350
divisor with multiplicity. Over the
integers, multiplicity is hard to find;

93
00:06:24,990 --> 00:06:27,290
it is something like
differentiating whole numbers.

94
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:32,230
Its approach is to chain theta lifts,
each one mimicking multiplicity.

95
00:06:32,850 --> 00:06:35,950
For a von Mangoldt sum of length X,
the note says,

96
00:06:36,610 --> 00:06:39,100
Claude suggested a chain
of about root X lifts.

97
00:06:39,850 --> 00:06:42,630
In the source,
the prime sum is exactly the note's,

98
00:06:43,190 --> 00:06:45,370
and a single theta lift
is a stated theorem.

99
00:06:46,330 --> 00:06:48,790
The article has not located
the chain or its length;

100
00:06:49,490 --> 00:06:51,480
no name in the tree contains Stepanov.

101
00:06:54,550 --> 00:06:59,290
An independent check of the source has
now been reported. The full build passes,

102
00:06:59,710 --> 00:07:02,070
the proof uses only Lean's
three standard axioms,

103
00:07:02,850 --> 00:07:06,850
Lean's kernel replays every module,
and independent kernels accept the proof.

104
00:07:07,710 --> 00:07:11,730
Andrew Sutherland of MIT reported
this on 21 November 2026;

105
00:07:12,350 --> 00:07:13,460
some runs are still going.

106
00:07:13,970 --> 00:07:15,450
These checks certify one thing:

107
00:07:16,190 --> 00:07:18,310
Lean accepts this proof
of exactly this statement.

108
00:07:18,830 --> 00:07:22,250
It does not show that Mathlib's
definitions mean the classical ones,

109
00:07:22,250 --> 00:07:26,380
and it does not explain the argument.
Both need expert reading.

110
00:07:26,930 --> 00:07:31,070
On 20 November, Ravi Vakil posted
on his blog an independent,

111
00:07:31,350 --> 00:07:35,570
human-written manuscript of about
940 pages by Alessandro Masullo,

112
00:07:36,290 --> 00:07:38,410
claiming a proof of the
generalized Riemann hypothesis;

113
00:07:39,170 --> 00:07:40,990
Brian Conrad had reviewed it for months.

114
00:07:41,520 --> 00:07:45,410
In Mathlib's form, the Riemann hypothesis
now has a machine-checked proof.

115
00:07:45,990 --> 00:07:49,290
What remains open is the human question:
what the argument is,

116
00:07:49,550 --> 00:07:50,820
and how much of it is needed.

